          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Cordelia\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as an existing emotional and relational state. The surrounding qualification limits its extent but does not change the focal expression from an explicit avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.83,\"reason\":\"The focal avowal is explicitly tied to Cordelia’s existing filial bond, thereby invoking the love and duties she owes Lear as his daughter. This is not clearly a new promise or undertaking; it chiefly affirms an existing obligation and her adherence to it.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The expression is a controlled, propositional avowal qualified by the measure of Cordelia’s bond, not a reflex-like or affectively discharged cry. Her difficulty putting her heart into words indicates emotion but does not make the focal words an expressive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"The expression’s core force is adequately represented by avowal together with its contextual invocation of filial obligation. Its restrained contrast with her sisters’ rhetoric does not require a separate residual force for the focal expression itself.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear asks his daughters to say how much they love him while linking their answers to the division of his kingdom. Cordelia answers Lear directly and refuses an unlimited or embellished profession, avowing love only according to her filial bond. Lear treats the answer as inadequate and asks her to amend it, warning that it may damage her fortunes.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes rather than newly creates her filial undertaking: as Lear’s daughter, she owes him fitting love, obedience, and honour. The exact practical terms are not specified. Lear does not accept her measured formulation as an adequate response; he challenges her to revise it.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from Shakespeare’s King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence supports Cordelia’s avowal and supplies her own statements that her love exceeds her speech. It does not independently establish every aspect of sincerity, the full prior relationship, or the eventual fulfilment of the invoked duties.\"}"
        }
      ],
